Keybinds: {
	Debug.DisplayName: Open the randomizer debug panel
	Journal.DisplayName: Open the randomizer codex
}

Debug: {
	Title: Ultimate Randomizer - debug
	NeedWorld: Load a world first.
	SinglePlayerOnly: This action only works in single player.
	TabOverview: Overview
	TabInspector: Inspector
	TabTools: Tools
	TabTests: Tests
	On: on
	Off: off
	Seed: Seed
	Mode: Mode
	Network: Game
	SinglePlayer: single player
	Multiplayer: multiplayer
	Tier: Unlocked tier
	Limit: Max allowed tier
	Gating: Lock
	Pool: Items randomizable / protected / excluded
	Snapshots: Drop tables enemies / bags
	Findable: Found from tier {0}, obtainable (with crafting) from tier {1}
	GuaranteedOn: Guaranteed drop of: {0}
	GuaranteedTag: (guaranteed)
	Individual: "{0}: health x{1}, damage x{2}, defense x{3}, size x{4}; loot bonus x{5}"
	Adaptive: Adaptive power: health x{0}, damage x{1} (included above)
	Power: Adaptive power
	PowerStats: power {0}, gap {1} tiers: enemies health x{2} damage x{3}, bosses health x{4} damage x{5}
	KindNormal: Normal
	KindGiant: Giant
	KindMini: Mini
	Swaps: Swaps
	SwapStats: "{0} flagged: {1} enemies and {2} critters swapped; {3} not swappable, {4} kept by chance, {5} without a replacement, {6} in slots not open yet"
	Recipes: Recipes
	Shimmer: Shimmer
	Chests: Chests
	SetTier: Set progression (boss flags) to tier:
	NewSeed: New random seed
	PoolTable: Items per category, tiers 0 to 9:
	TierSet: Progression set to {0}.
	SeedSet: New seed: {0}.
	HoverItem: Item under the mouse (or held)
	NoItem: Hover an item or hold one.
	ItemInfo: Category {0}, tier {1} ({2}), protected {3}, excluded {4}, stack {5}-{6}, value {7}
	ShufflePartner: Guaranteed shuffle partner: {0}
	NearestNpc: NPC nearest to the mouse
	NoNpc: No NPC near the mouse.
	NpcTier: tier {0} (rolls: {1})
	Multipliers: Multipliers: life x{0}, damage x{1}, defense x{2}, size x{3}
	Swappable: Can be replaced by the random enemies feature.
	NotSwappable: Never replaced by the random enemies feature.
	Drops: Drops in this world:
	NoDrops: Drops nothing in this world.
	ExtraTag: (extra)
	ConditionTag: (conditions)
	Player: Player
	GodMode: Invincible
	SpawnRate: Spawns
	SpawnRateNormal: normal
	SpawnRateNone: none
	SpawnRateHigh: many
	ToggleGodMode: Invincible on/off
	CycleSpawnRate: Spawn rate
	GiveKit: Test kit
	GiveEndgameKit: Endgame kit
	World: World
	Day: day
	Night: night
	SpawnEnemies: Spawn 5 enemies
	KillHostiles: Kill enemies on screen
	ToggleDay: Day/night
	GiveBags: All boss bags
	OpenJournal: Codex
	ToolsHelp: Tip: Inspector tab + mouse over an enemy shows its drops; kill it to compare. Spawned enemies spawn like natural ones (random enemies apply).
	RunTests: Run all tests
	TestsHelp: Runs every automated test in the current world. Results also go to client.log.
	TestSummary: "{0} passed, {1} failed, {2} skipped"
}

Tests: {
	NoWorld: World loaded
	PoolBuilt: Item pool built, every main category has items
	Classification: Known items are classified correctly
	Determinism: Rolls are deterministic and depend on the seed
	GatingRespected: No item above the allowed tier (progression lock)
	NoProtectedRolled: Protected and excluded items are never given at random
	SameCategory: Replacements keep the category
	ShuffleBijection: Guaranteed shuffle: every obtainable item is given exactly once
	StackRules: Stack sizes are valid
	PriceFloor: Shop prices never resell for more than they cost, and never go below the minimum price (the stackable one for a stackable item)
	DropRates: Drop rates are valid
	TierFromRecipes: Crafted items take the tier of their vanilla recipe when it is earlier than their rarity (Tinkerer combinations)
	StorageKitOnce: The Magic Storage kit is given once per world, and again in another world
	SnapshotsCaptured: Original drop tables were read
	MobPlansValid: Every enemy drop table is valid
	LootAbundanceScales: Loot abundance scales enemy drops, not guaranteed ones
	DropTableLength: Enemies get long drop tables (2 to 7 slots: about 1.85 times the former 1 to 4)
	JackpotUseful: Jackpots are always useful items
	JackpotNotInEnemyDrops: Jackpots are not listed among the enemy's drops
	JackpotRatesLower: Jackpots are a little rarer by default
	JackpotDropsMarked: Drops above the limit are marked as jackpots
	GiantBonusUseful: A giant's bonus item is useful, and five times as likely to be a jackpot
	MiniBonusDrop: Minis and giants give one bonus item, boss minions never
	JackpotWorthScales: Weak enemies get a share of the jackpot chances that follows their health (0.1 to 1)
	SpawnedEnemyNoExtraDrops: Enemies spawned by another enemy give no extra drop, guaranteed drop or jackpot
	CrittersNoJackpot: Critters never give a jackpot
	BagNeverJackpot: Opened bags and crates never give a jackpot
	JackpotTail: Each extra tier of a jackpot is much rarer, and only jackpots go above the limit (hardmode included)
	HardmodeWall: Nothing from hardmode before hardmode except jackpots and boss floors, hints consistent with the limit
	RepickOnlyChangesFlagged: The re-pick only replaces banned or too advanced items, every other roll is unchanged
	RaisedTiers: Crafted items that need hardmode materials wait for hardmode, nothing is lowered
	BannedNeverRolled: Creative and internal items (tModLoader, Recipe Browser, creative items of other mods) are never given
	ChestCap: Chest content never goes above the chest's cap, nor reaches hardmode in a pre-hardmode chest
	ChestTameKeepsOpened: Taming the chests of an ongoing world never touches an opened or jackpot chest
	JackpotChests: Jackpot chests are chosen the same way every time among eligible chests and hold useful items above their cap
	PlanDiff: Measures how much of this world's plan (recipes, findability, guaranteed drops) the hardmode wall changes, compared with the older rules (logged); the final drops of enemies and bags must not change
	TierCoherence: Measures, for the recipe redesign, the materials of each tier, the progression stations, the recipes lacking an ingredient of their result's tier, the raised tiers and the jackpot chest tail (logged)
	TierAudit: Lists the vanilla items whose tier looks doubtful (raised by their sources, a source much earlier, or no source at all) in TierAudit.tsv, and counts the banned items of the enemy plan (logged)
	ItemTierDump: Writes ItemTiers.tsv, one row per vanilla item with its tiers, findability, vanilla and planned recipe tiers, planned ingredients and mining data (read-only)
	WorldChestTrueTiers: Items of world-generated chests are found from their chest's tier (true tier lowered, never raised)
	ShopDump: Writes Shops.tsv, every vanilla shop entry at each unlocked tier with its replacement (a random roll), then the added slots selling this world's shop-only items, with price, conditions and flags (too early, other category, shop-only item lost), and ShopOnly.tsv, which merchant sells each shop-only item of this world and from which tier (read-only)
	WorldExport: Writes items, recipes, drops, bags, shops, chests, worldsources and findability .tsv files plus a README into Export/ next to the other dumps, for an offline coherence audit (read-only)
	ShopsNotTooEarly: Shop replacements never go above the unlocked tier + 1 + the shop's tier jitter, no weapon, armor, accessory or tool goes above the unlocked tier, and no added slot appears before its item's unlock tier
	ShopOnlyItemsKept: Items only shops sell in vanilla (wiring, Clentaminator and solutions, biome seeds, bombs, storage...) stay obtainable in this world: found or crafted at any tier, or sold in an added slot of a reliable merchant (late sales are only logged)
	ShopOnlyPlacement: Every shop-only item of this world (sold by a random shop entry, and neither found nor crafted anywhere in this world, at any tier) has exactly one added slot at a reliable merchant (known arrival, not Santa, the Princess or a visitor), shown from its unlock tier, without exceeding a shop's free capacity while others have room, never rolled again in that shop, the same for the same seed (late merchants are logged)
	ShopOnlySlots: Each shop with added slots, filled as the game fills it right now and given its added slots as in game, shows every unlocked shop-only item at its price, and only a rolled slot ever gives way when the shop is full
	ShopNoDuplicates: With no duplicates in shops, no merchant sells the same replacement twice, all its tabs together
	ShopPricesAffordable: Shop prices stay between their floor (half the value, the minimum price) and their tier's ceiling, items without a value other than gear cost a few copper or silver, and cheap stackable items keep their small price
	ShopSpread: No randomized item is sold by more merchants than the limit of the settings at any tier, apart from a few that a small category leaves no choice
	ShopPylonsGuaranteed: The nine teleportation pylons and the Red Solution each have an added slot at a reliable merchant, whatever the world
	ShopAddedSlotsOnTime: No added slot opens before the later of its item's tier and true tier minus the tier allowance
	ModdedRecipesRandomized: With Magic Storage, its recipes are rebuilt with a key of their tier, and its storage upgrades' tiers follow their bars in order
	ModdedItemsInPool: Magic Storage's items may be given at random; the items of utility mods (tModLoader, Recipe Browser) and creative items never are
	ModdedDropsPermuted: The drops of other mods' enemies and bosses are permuted with everyone's, at their estimated tier
	ModdedStationsTiered: Other mods' stations get the tier of their item, are only given to recipes of their tier, and the themed ones are guaranteed when unprotected
	TierFloorsHold: The audited tier floors hold: hard gates without allowance, the Creeper as its Brain, late enemies and global drops, lock boxes, mining levels, reliable sources lowering
	TiersConsistent: Every item's tier is its true tier, and the audited tier errors stay fixed
	GuaranteeScalesWithPlayers: Guaranteed stations are shared between players, random stations are rarer
	LockedDropsRedistributed: The chances of locked drops go to the unlocked random drops of the enemy, capped at 100%
	LockedVanillaDropRemoved: A replaced original drop still locked by progression does not drop; an unlocked one drops as planned
	DropPermutationConserves: The drop permutation loses and duplicates nothing: every permuted vanilla drop is still dropped by some enemy
	DropPermutationMoves: Permuted drops stay within their tier group and most of them move to another enemy
	DropPermutationAccess: Expert-only, master-only and special-seed drops only move to places with the same restrictions
	DropPermutationRuntime: Each enemy's drop rules really follow the permutation: silenced vanilla drops are rolled somewhere, nothing drops twice, variants keep their own drops, enemies without drops get one
	DropPermutationStable: Removing one drop entry from the permutation only changes the content of very few other entries
	DropPermutationSurprise: Permutation surprises replace about the set share of the shuffled drops, never a material nor the copy every item but blocks, critters and ores keeps
	PermutationDuplicatesCapped: An item keeps at most its capped number of shuffled copies per drop condition (the others become surprises), and always at least one in each
	PermutationKeepsStartDrops: With the default caps, Gel stays among the start materials (the Lens rate is only logged)
	ExtraStacksSmall: Random extra drops and surprises come in small stacks: potions and food a few, ammo by dozens, keys and summons one at a time
	GatesNotOnLowerOwners: No random extra drop nor surprise gives a hard gate, key or summon above the tier of its enemy, except a jackpot
	StrongEnemiesDropEarly: Strong enemies coming with hardmode drop their permuted loot from hardmode on; Martian enemies drop at the Martian tier
	BossPermutationConserves: The boss permutation loses and duplicates nothing: every shuffled boss drop and treasure bag content is still in some boss or bag
	BossBagMatchesBoss: Each treasure bag holds the shuffled normal-mode drops of its boss, with their chance and stack in the bag they come from
	BossPermutationTiers: Boss drops only move between bosses of the same group of tiers, with the same restrictions
	WorldDropsReported: With the permutation, the bestiary and the drop reports show the world's drops: what an enemy drops now, not its silenced vanilla rules nor the entries progression still locks
	BossRuntime: The bosses' and bags' drop rules really follow the permutation: silenced drops are rolled somewhere, nothing drops twice
	PermutationConservation: Every vanilla drop entry of the enemies, bosses and boss bags is somewhere exactly once, at its place or moved
	PermutedConditions: Moved drops only carry conditions they can meet: no seasonal or first-kill condition, item conditions stay with their item
	EaterOfWorldsParts: The Eater of Worlds' body and tail segments drop from the head's table, with the boss floor
	BossGroupsMerge: Small consecutive boss tiers group together first, and the Moon Lord's loot stays with the late bosses
	CodexShowsLocked: Codex interface: locked drops are greyed with the boss that unlocks them, with no chance
	ProtectedDropsKept: Protected drops, coins, bags and upgrades are kept
	NoMinionFarms: Boss minions and town NPCs get no random drops
	EventItemsKept: Old One's Army items and boss segments are handled
	SwapSurvivesVariantSetDefaults: Enemy variants (Pinky, zombie variants) are still swapped
	CritterSwapLive: Critters really get swapped, in the same habitat
	CritterPoolSafe: No gold critter, Prismatic Lacewing, Truffle Worm or lava critter can appear in place of another
	CritterHabitat: Water, flying and ground critters stay in their habitat
	SwapStatsCount: Natural spawns are counted, statue spawns are left alone
	NpcTiersNormalStats: Enemy tiers come from normal-mode stats, whatever world was played before a reload
	CodexRecordsLoot: The codex records the kills and every item dropped by a common enemy with a sure drop (a slime when none)
	CodexRecipesFromGuide: The Guide discovers the recipes using the item and those giving it
	CodexSaveLoad: The codex is saved and loaded with the world without loss
	CodexSyncRoundTrip: Codex discoveries survive the network format (delta and full sync)
	CodexUiBuilds: Codex interface: every tab, item, enemy and craft tree page builds
	CodexHidesUndiscovered: Codex interface: undiscovered recipes, enemies, items and bags stay hidden
	CodexManyItemsBounded: Codex interface: 3000 items and 300 enemies only build the visible rows
	CodexTextsResolved: Codex interface: no raw text key is shown
	CodexShowsChances: Codex interface: discovered drops show their planned chance, biomes the spawn shares
	CodexShowsConditions: The codex shows when an enemy is met (by day, at night) on its biome, enemy and item pages
	BiomeSlotsReachable: By biome: every spawn place of an enemy with several places can be reached
	NegativeNetIdSwap: Swapping into a slime colour (Pinky) works and keeps the Blue Slime type
	EvilSlotsShared: By biome: in a world with a single evil, the owners of the missing evil's places spawn through the present evil (20 seeds)
	BiomeRedistributionComplete: By biome: every natural enemy keeps its number of spawn places, inside its class and its tier pair, never more than one tier above the place (20 seeds)
	BiomeSlotsRespectDaylight: By biome: no enemy that flees or dies in daylight (Gnome) in a day surface place
	BiomeExtrasShare: By biome: 8% of the spawn places are shared with event enemies
	CriticalHostsInForest: By biome: the guardians of the first stations are enemies met in the first hour (Forest, caves or desert)
	SurpriseZero: With 0% surprise, replaced items keep their category
	SurpriseHundred: With 100% surprise, categories follow the loot weights
	SurpriseGatedAndProtected: Surprises respect progression and protected items
	WeightTablesSeparated: Loot weights favor equipment, ingredient weights never pick it
	TierWindowVariety: Early random rolls give many different items
	SparseBucketsWiden: A sparse tier of a category widens to the tiers below for variety
	OreMappingBijective: Each ore of this world still exists, as the drop of exactly one other ore of the world; absent ores stay themselves, and deciding a hardmode pair changes no ore of another tier; one-to-one even with both ores of a pair present
	TreeShakeReplacement: Shaken trees give random items and keep acorns
	HerbReplacementTable: Each herb has a fixed set of valid replacements
	HerbTableDistinct: A herb's replacement table never holds the same item twice
	ExtractinatorRules: The Extractinator keeps coins and swaps ores for ores
	AnglerRewardsValid: Angler rewards are replaced by valid items, coins kept
	TreeCritterSwap: Critters falling from trees are swapped too
	PacingExpected: 15 to 25 pieces of random equipment per early hour (default settings; the value is written to client.log)
	PacingSimulation: The pacing simulation is stable and regular
	PacingVariety: At least 10 different pieces of equipment per early hour
	JackpotPacing: Few jackpots in an early hour (logged per hour)
	DropFrequency: Most kills give something (at least 0.6 items per kill; the value is written to client.log)
	WorkBenchPacing: The work bench is made by hand at the start, and its guardian alone gives one within an hour on average (written to client.log)
	BasicStationsUnprotected: Basic stations (work bench, furnace, anvil...) are left to the randomizer
	ProgressionStationsProtected: Progression and themed stations are protected unless station protection is None
	ProgressionStationsGuaranteed: Unprotected progression stations (from their guardian) and boss summons drop from frequent enemies of their tier (1 % or more)
	ProtectionsRemoved: Progression materials, mirrors, summons and progression tools are randomized, still found at their tier, and no permutation moves a hard gate below its tier
	AbsentEvilBoss: The evil boss a world lacks takes no part in the boss permutation and is no source
	TempleKeyOutsideTemple: The Temple Key is never guaranteed in the Temple nor on enemies met only there
	ConfigMigration: Old saved settings: an old default list of protected items is removed, the player's choices kept
	UpgradesOutOfPools: Life and mana upgrades are never rolled, guaranteed or used as ingredients, and drops keep them
	BasicStationsFindable: Every basic station can be found at the start (its guardian), never in pots
	GuaranteedReachability: Every guaranteed item is carried by natural enemies of its tier, or by the progression bosses of its tier
	GuaranteedBiomeHosts: Each guaranteed item is carried by several enemies of one biome, with the same chance on each (a station: its single guardian)
	GuaranteedSeveralSeeds: Every guaranteed item gets the enemies of a biome with other seeds
	GuaranteedBiomesSpread: The guaranteed items of a tier are spread over several biomes
	GuaranteedBiomesVaried: The first stations land in at least five different nearby biomes, never in a far one
	GuaranteedFindTime: Every guaranteed summon and tool drops within about 90 kills of its biome (the station guardians are logged)
	GuaranteedDayAndNight: A guaranteed summon or tool whose biome is met by day and by night has at least two enemies at each time
	GuaranteedHostLoad: No enemy carries more than four guaranteed items (five when its biome has no other choice), and none gives too many guaranteed drops per kill
	GuaranteedReachable: Guaranteed items never sit in a biome before it can be farmed (Dungeon, Underworld, Temple, sky), nor in the Graveyard or the Meteor
	HintSortedByProgress: Hints are sorted by progression, in two tabs
	HintFoundUnlocks: A found item's hint is fully known for free
	HintClickOpensCodex: Clicking a hint opens the right codex page
	HintTreeComplete: A recipe hint names the source of every ingredient and station, down to raw materials
	HintTreeDeterministic: Recipe hint trees are the same after rebuilding
	HintTabsSplit: The Guide's hints are split between crafting and bosses
	HintTreeKeepsOtherHints: A hint tree keeps the stations and summons of other hints hidden until they are known
	HintConditionsShown: Hints and craft trees say when a source works: time, weather or event, drop and recipe conditions, altars for hardmode ores
	HintSummonTiers: Boss summons are offered when their boss is the next step (Power Cell after Plantera, Mechanical Eye in hardmode)
	HintLockedShown: Hints for later are listed greyed with when they come, and are never sold
	StationHints: The last hint about a station tells both its recipe (ingredients and stations) and its guardian
	GuaranteedHostsNotEvents: Guaranteed drops are never on invasion, moon, seasonal or weather enemies
	GuaranteedDeterminism: Guaranteed sources are the same after a rebuild
	FindabilityDeterminism: Findability of items is the same after a rebuild
	WorldMaterialsFindable: Wood, stone, gems and early ores are findable from the start
	BagContentFindable: Bag contents count as found only once the bag can be obtained (never seasonal bags, treasure bags only in expert mode)
	CrateAccessWorld: Crates the world cannot give (other evil, pre-hardmode Hallowed crate) are no source
	HardmodeOresOfWorld: Hardmode pair ores always count as findable and are never new ingredients
	AltarDecisionKeepsCaches: An altar choosing a hardmode ore changes neither the guaranteed sources nor findability
	PotOnlyUnfound: Pots only give guaranteed items nobody has found yet
	GuaranteesNotBelowTier: No guaranteed item comes before its tier (Ancient Manipulator and Celestial Sigil from the Lunatic Cultist on)
	StationsOneGuardian: Every crafting station has exactly one guardian, a common enemy of its tier, and never comes from pots or chests
	BiomeKeysGuaranteed: The biome keys of this world are guaranteed on hardmode enemies and found from hardmode on
	GuardiansInThisWorld: Station guardians are enemies this world really spawns, never only in the evil biome it lacks
	IngredientReplacementsFindable: New ingredients are had (found or crafted) by the tier their recipe was rebuilt for
	RecipesDoable: At least 85% of the randomized recipes can be made at their level
	AvailabilityConsistent: The planned item availability matches an independent recomputation over the applied recipes
	NoSelfIngredient: No recipe uses its own result
	ResultNotInGroup: No randomized recipe gives an item its own ingredient group accepts
	IngredientValueCoherent: Rebuilt recipes are worth at most twice the vanilla ingredients (cheaper is fine: the amounts follow the vanilla amounts)
	ProtectedIngredientsKept: Protected ingredients are never replaced
	RecipeGroupsSane: Every recipe group (any wood, any iron bar...) still has its ingredient
	RecipePlanDeterminism: Ingredients and stations are the same after a rebuild
	UntouchedRecipesIntact: Protected recipes keep their ingredients and stations
	DecraftSafeWithIngredients: Shimmer decrafting never gives back more valuable ingredients
	VanillaModeGuaranteed: In vanilla ingredient mode, every missing ingredient is placed on enemies
	StationDag: No crafting station needs itself to be made, directly or through other stations
	StationTierRespected: Recipes never need a station above their level, nor a themed one
	HandCraftKept: Recipes made by hand stay made by hand
	StationsVaried: A good share of recipes changed station
	AppliedResultsMatchPlan: Recipes give their vanilla result or their swap partner's, as planned, and the availability matches the live recipes
	StationPlanFromOriginals: The station plan depends only on the original recipes, not on the already randomized ones
	RealCrafting: The crafting menu offers a recipe with its new ingredients, and no longer with its old ones
	PickaxePerMiningLevel: Each mining level has a pickaxe by the tier of its ores (crafted, found or guaranteed)
	RecipeRandomizationShare: Nearly every vanilla recipe changes (95%), few swap their result (about the swap chance); logs the reasons
	RecipeEveryItemKept: Every item a vanilla recipe makes is still made by a recipe, and nothing had in vanilla is lost (Zenith)
	RecipeNoCycles: No item is made from itself through a new ingredient, directly or through other recipes
	RecipeKeyed: Every rebuilt recipe above tier 0 has a key and can't be made before its tier
	RecipeSwapsShare: About the swap chance of the recipes swap their results, by pairs of the same tier, mostly of different kinds
	RecipeSwapCancelClean: A cancelled swap pair leaves the candidates exactly as before, and its recipes give their own result again
	ZenithWeapons: The Zenith keeps weapons among its ingredients
	IngredientReuse: The reuse penalty spreads the materials over more recipes
	CraftedValueFromIngredients: An item crafted through a randomized recipe sells for at most its ingredients, through saves, network, splits and stacks
	ShimmerNoMoneyLoop: A cheaply crafted item never shimmers into more money than it was worth
	ShimmerLiveNoMoneyLoop: A real shimmer of cheaply crafted items never gives more value than it consumed
	ProtectedRecipesDoable: Recipes making progression stations and protected items can be made at their level
	RecipeKeyIngredient: Randomized recipes can't be made before the tier of their result (key ingredient)
	RecipeNoStationTwins: The same recipe (result and ingredients) never shows on two different stations
	UpgradeRecipes: Each life or mana upgrade has its own recipe, doable from its tier, with a key ingredient and ingredients worth at least the upgrade
	HouseItemsObtainable: Door, platform and torch are hint targets, and their recipes change but are craftable at once (tier 0 ingredients, basic station)
	RecipesRebuilt: At least 90% of the recipes with replaceable ingredients get new ingredients
	RecipeIngredientCount: A rebuilt recipe has the vanilla number of ingredients give or take the set spread, within the set maximum
	RecipeIngredientTiers: A rebuilt recipe has no ingredient above its tier, and most have one of exactly its tier
	RecipeIngredientsAcyclic: Every crafted ingredient of a rebuilt recipe is made by a recipe planned before it
	RecipeIngredientUseCaps: No material is an ingredient of more rebuilt recipes than its use cap
	RecipeExamples: Logs 10 rebuilt recipes (Zenith and Chlorophyte armor among them), which really changed
	UncraftableRecipesValid: Items no recipe makes get a live recipe, doable from their tier and never before, that never shimmers back; logs examples
	UncraftableRecipesOff: With the setting off (planner called with explicit parameters), no shell recipe is planned and the other recipes are planned the same
	UncraftableNoSpecials: Shell recipes keep their item (an upgrade only its own), are never swapped and never switched off as copies
	UncraftablePricedByIngredients: Live shell recipes count as randomized: what they make sells for its ingredients' value, not its vanilla price
	RecipePickaxeMineable: No rebuilt pickaxe or drill recipe gets a new ingredient that needs at least that pick power to be mined
	RecipeNotBeforeItsTier: No rebuilt or swapped recipe is doable before the tier of the item it gives (its own tier, or its true tier when later): no Chlorophyte Pickaxe in early hardmode
	RecipeValueSane: Planned recipes ask for sane stacks (their count cap, or three times a vanilla stack) and rebuilt ones are worth at most twice their vanilla recipe (value-0 items count their own recipe): no 9999 Ruby Walls for a Mana Crystal, no Nebula Fragment kept for a cheap swapped item
	ProtectedRecipesGated: A protected result whose ingredients are rebuilt waits for the unlocked tier of its true tier like any other: no Key of Night or Key of Light before hardmode
	PickaxeIngredientsBelowLevel: A pickaxe or drill recipe never gets an ore or bar (or anything made of one) that needs its own pick power or more to be mined: no Orichalcum Pickaxe made of Chlorophyte Bars
	StationsCraftable: Every crafting station has a rebuilt recipe of found materials, doable by its tier without its guardian; the Work Bench by hand at the start
	HouseRecipesSimple: Torch, Wood Platform and Wooden Door have a simple random recipe: one or two start materials, a few of each, worth at most twice the vanilla recipe, at a basic station or by hand
	IngredientCountCaps: No new ingredient asks for more than its count cap: a few of a crafted-only item, about ten of a slow one (gems, Fallen Stars, herbs, fish, critters, shards, souls)
	IngredientCountsFromVanilla: The new ingredients of a rebuilt recipe count at most two and a half times the vanilla ingredients they replace (logs the share of amounts at the cap and the Meteorite Bar's recipe)
	NoNeverIngredients: No live planned recipe needs an ingredient that is never had (an Ice Mirror, a Marshmallow); a recipe kept vanilla gets a replacement for it
	NoEmptyLiveRecipes: No live recipe has an empty ingredient list (a free craft)
	SwapStationsInTime: A swapped result never lands on a station later than its own tier and the allowance (no Tungsten Bow at the Lunar Crafting Station)
	BellCurveShape: Enemy stats are bell-shaped: mostly normal, a few % far from it
	BellCurveBounds: Rolled stats always stay inside their range
	ExtremenessEffect: The rarity setting makes extreme values rarer
	GiantMiniFrequency: Giants and minis appear as often as set
	GiantMiniBounds: Giant and mini sizes stay inside their range
	GiantHitboxCap: Giants never exceed the largest allowed size
	IndividualSyncRoundTrip: Individual stats (giant, mini...) reach multiplayer clients intact
	ClientIndividualLife: Giants and minis have the right maximum health on multiplayer clients, without resetting their synced health
	NoGiantMinions: Boss minions are never giants or minis and never give the giant bonus drop
	TransformRerollsIndividual: Swapped enemies get their own individual stats
	VariantGetsIndividual: An enemy turned into a variant (Pinky...) keeps its own individual stats and health ratio
	TransformKeepsIndividual: A vanilla transformation keeps the enemy's individual stats (no giant flicker) and its health ratio
	StatCacheBossFlag: Boss multipliers do not depend on an earlier lookup of the same type as a non-boss
	LootBonusCap: The loot bonus grows with strength and stays under its cap
	LootBonusApplied: Stronger enemies get a loot bonus
	DamageCapRespected: Enemy damage multipliers stay under the total damage cap
	AdaptivePowerMeasured: Player power comes from the best weapon, the worn armor and the 3 best accessories
	AdaptiveFormula: Adaptive multipliers grow with the gap, are capped and never below x1
	AdaptiveAppliedAtSpawn: Enemies get the adaptive multipliers when they spawn
	AdaptiveBossParts: Boss parts are scaled like their boss
	AdaptiveNotCompounded: A transformed enemy keeps its adaptive multipliers without stacking them
	BagPlansValid: Every bag content is valid
	BagsKeepCoins: Boss bags keep their coins
	CrittersDrop: Critters get their share of the small frequent drop
	WildTail: The madness tail gives the expected share above the limit, rarer for each tier
	PlansIgnoreWild: Plans, findability and guarantees don't depend on the madness tail
	RecipesSafe: Recipes: no self-ingredient, value guard respected
	DecraftSafe: Shimmer decrafting can't duplicate items
	ShimmerCoherent: Shimmer transformations keep category and value
	ShopSimulation: Simulated shop: no duplicates, fair prices, special slots untouched
	EnemySwapPool: Random enemies: no boss parts, minions or event enemies
	EnemySwapDaylight: Random enemies: nothing that flees daylight by day
	TownOrderPermutation: Town NPC order is a valid permutation
	EnemyStatRanges: Boss multipliers stay in the configured range
	ConfigTexts: Every setting has a text
	OtherTexts: Every message has a text
	ConfigRanges: Settings are consistent
	DebugToolsDefaultOff: Debug tools are off by default
	CacheSignatureScope: Saving an enemy stat setting keeps findability and the recipe plan; loot and swap pool settings rebuild them
	CacheSignatureCovers: Every spawn setting the plans read (spawn mode, biome extras, swap chances) and progression scaling rebuild the world caches
	ConfigResetDefaults: Settings: the reset button restores every default and covers every config
	HintFactsTrue: Hints say what the planners do: the host carries the item, the recipe makes it
	HintPriceScales: Hint prices follow the formula and rise with the level and the tier
	HintPurchaseFlow: Buying a hint takes the coins, raises its level, and is refused when coins are short
	HintSyncRoundTrip: Hint levels survive the codex wire format, the save and the server checks
	HintTextsResolved: Every hint sentence is a full sentence in this language
	HintSourceFrequent: Creature hints lead to enemies met often (5 % of a biome or more, together for a guaranteed item) with a 1 % chance or more
	HintTargetsHaveSource: Every guaranteed station and summon has a creature or recipe hint, never a rare fallback
	HintBiomeEveryLevel: Levels 2 to 4 of a creature hint name its best biome
	HintRevealDiscovers: The last level of a hint reveals its enemies in the codex (page, drop, biome), saved and synced
	HintHostsNamed: A guaranteed item's hint names every enemy of its biome carrying it, with the chance on each
	PlannedShareSums: The planned spawn shares of each biome sum to 100 %, rare enemies get less
}

Journal.Description: Opens the randomizer codex (also the J key)

